+## Limits
+
+- **No canvas IME** (#322). The Type tool on the canvas reads `egui::Event::Text`, `Paste`,
+  `Copy`/`Cut` and `Key` only (`canvas.rs`); it doesn't handle `egui::Event::Ime` (`Enabled`,
+  `Preedit`, `Commit`, `Disabled`) and never sets `PlatformOutput::ime`, so the window doesn't
+  ask for IME input and Hangul can't be composed on the canvas. Text fields, the Notes panel and
+  the Story Editor (Edit › Edit in Story Editor) are egui `TextEdit`s, which handle IME
+  themselves, so Korean should compose there; by egui's `TextEdit` design each Story Editor
+  composition (preedit) step would reach the story as an edit (not yet checked on Windows). Use the Story Editor or paste Korean text until the canvas IME lands.
+- Without a craft-fonts Hangul face and without an installed one (Linux without Korean fonts,
+  the web build) Hangul in the UI shows as boxes. Other interface languages don't load the
+  installed Hangul face, so the 한국어 item of the language menu shows boxes there unless the
+  build embeds a Hangul face (as 日本語 and 简体中文 do without craft-fonts).
+- The Korean UI's bold (semibold) text uses the regular Hangul face when it comes from the system.
+- The web build doesn't read the browser's language.
+- Native-speaker review: the catalog was written and revised by machine translation agents
+  against Korean InDesign terminology; it has not had a human review yet.
